<p>In 1878, the Northern Pacific Railway decided to resume construction of its main line west to the Yellowstone River near Glendive, Montana. The company needed administrative offices and a passenger depot west of the Missouri River.</p>

<p>Under the direction of lead engineer {% person "thomas-rosser" %}Thomas Rosser{% endperson %}, a former Confederate general associated with Fort Lincoln, the railway constructed a one-story, gable-roofed depot measuring 24 by 70 feet and surrounded by a 12-foot-wide platform. Three years later, the building became a freight warehouse when a new passenger depot was constructed. The original depot was sold and moved in 1912.</p>

