    <figcaption>Queen Anne depot under construction, 1882; Gilbert/Miller photograph</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The New York architectural firm McKim, Mead &amp; White designed the replacement. The firm also designed the original portion of the White House's West Wing and the New York City mansion of Northern Pacific president {% person "henry-villard" %}Henry Villard{% endperson %}.</p>

<p>The 100-by-32-foot depot had a brick first floor and a wood-framed second floor with hipped dormers, topped by a bell-shaped cupola. Designed in the Queen Anne style, it was surrounded by an eight-foot-wide veranda supported by ornate iron pillars. Its 400-foot trackside platform could accommodate as many as eight passenger cars.</p>

<p>The ground floor, level with the platform, accommodated employees and travelers. It contained a 14-by-30-foot express room, 13-by-18-foot ticket office, and 14-by-30-foot baggage room. In keeping with the period's custom, a 29-by-39-foot waiting room for women and children was separated from the 29-by-30-foot men's waiting room so that women would not be exposed to the “rough language” considered common among men.</p>

