    <figcaption>Mandan's Queen Anne depot, circa 1907 postcard</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The second floor housed railway management offices and a Western Union telegraph office staffed by as many as nine operators around the clock. A battery room held lead-acid wet-cell jars that supplied backup electrical power. East of the depot stood a plain one-story building containing the dining room and lunch counter, commonly called the “beanery.” The depot was described as the finest west of St. Paul.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/northern-pacific-queen-anne-depot/708-post-fire-photo.jpg" alt="Queen Anne depot after the June 1920 fire">
    <figcaption>Queen Anne depot after the June 1920 fire</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>A major fire in June 1920 began in defective electrical wiring, which likely ignited hydrogen released by batteries in the telegraph office. Furniture, tickets, and records were rescued from the first floor, but the second-floor telegraph, yardmaster, roadmaster, and trainmaster offices were destroyed. Temporary offices were established in spare cabooses parked east of the {% place "northern-pacific-beanery" %}beanery{% endplace %}.</p>

<p>Because the railroad was losing money, it rebuilt the second floor with a plain design, replacing the shingle siding with simple wooden siding. The depot remained in service until 1929, when the {% place "northern-pacific-colonial-depot" %}Colonial-style depot{% endplace %} replaced it.</p>
